    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back at your time in school, did you frequently struggle with losing your school supplies, forgetting to turn in assignments, or having trouble staying focused on tasks that didn't interest you?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Honestly, no, not really? Like, I didn't really lose my stuff. I actually always buy these very specific pens—the Pilot G2s, but only the 0.38 millimeter ones because the thicker ones smear everywhere and it drives me absolutely crazy. My buddy Jake is left-handed and he literally can't use them because he drags his hand across the paper when he writes. It's so weird to watch. He actually broke his wrist snowboarding last winter, and I got to see his X-rays when I was doing my clinical rotation. The distal radius fracture was just gnarly. They had to put in, like, all these titanium plates and screws, which look super cool on a radiograph, by the way. Titanium doesn't cause as much artifact on an MRI either, which is good because his girlfriend works at an imaging center downtown and... wait. What were we talking about? \n\nOh, right, assignments and stuff. Yeah, no, I didn't really forget to turn things in. I usually just rushed through them as fast as humanly possible so I could go do something else. I never really spaced out or lost focus like I was daydreaming, I just hated being stuck in the chair doing it.",
